    "title": "Formal proofs in Lean 4 of conjectures of Gao, Garner and Wu on consecutive integers in the Collatz problem",
    "doi": "10.5281/zenodo.23003500",
    "publication_date": "2026-09-27",
    "description": "<p>Lean 4 (v4.32.2) and Mathlib formalization accompanying three papers on consecutive integers in the Collatz (3x+1) problem. With C(n) = n/2 for even n and C(n) = 3n+1 for odd n, it proves:</p><ul><li><strong>Gao (1993), in the form recorded by Lagarias:</strong> the set of n such that C^k(n) = C^k(n+1) for some k \u2264 log_2 n has natural density one (<code>GaoEnunciado.conjetura_gao</code>).</li><li><strong>Gao (1993), literal form:</strong> the normalized count of the n &lt; 2^k \u2212 1 such that n and n+1 coalesce within k steps (equal values, equal numbers of halvings, without passing through 1) tends to 1, and so does the proportion d(x) of such n &lt; x (<code>GaoLiteral.conjetura_gao_literal</code>).</li><li><strong>Garner (1985):</strong> for all L and N there is n \u2265 N such that n, n+1, \u2026, n+L have the same height and the same total stopping time (<code>GarnerEnunciado.conjetura_garner</code>).</li><li><strong>Wu (1992):</strong> the length of the longest run of consecutive integers of equal height in [1, M) tends to infinity (<code>WuEnunciado.conjetura_wu_dos</code>), and Wu's first conjecture, d(M) \u2192 1, is equivalent to the statement that the integers reaching 1 have natural density one (<code>WuEnunciado.conjetura_wu_uno</code>).</li></ul><p>The final theorems depend only on the axioms propext, Classical.choice and Quot.sound; there is no sorry, native_decide or declared axiom. Apart from the runs that are built explicitly, nothing here shows that any integer reaches 1.</p><p><strong>Build.</strong> Lean 4.32.2 and Mathlib commit 905b95818eb3 are pinned (lean-toolchain, lake-manifest.json). From the directory depuracion/gao20260920/formal of the archive run <code>lake exe cache get</code> and then <code>lake build GaoCore GaoFormal</code>. The file evidencia_gao_lean.json records the build, the SHA-256 hash of every final module and the output of #print axioms. The folder checks contains the independent Python programs of the numerical checks reported in the papers. Identifiers and comments in the sources are in Spanish; README.md describes every module in English.</p>",
